::page{title="Get started" description="Open a site by its key, tabs, the desktop, install or bookmark." order=1}

# Get started

::toc

## Get HyperBrowser

Download it from [the releases page](https://github.com/tibocub/HyperBrowser/releases/latest). There is no installer to run.

- **Windows**: `HyperBrowser.zip`. Extract it (right-click, **Extract All…**) and put the `HyperBrowser` folder where it can stay (`Documents` for example). Double-click `HyperBrowser.exe` in it. Windows says "Windows protected your PC" because the files aren't signed: click **More info**, then **Run anyway**. The first start of a new version takes longer.
- **Linux**: `HyperBrowser.AppImage`. Put it where it can stay, make it executable (`chmod +x HyperBrowser.AppImage`) and run it. On Ubuntu 22.04 or newer, AppImages need `libfuse2` (`sudo apt install libfuse2`).

HyperBrowser updates itself, peer to peer: a new version downloads while it runs and starts the next time you open it. To start it right away, choose **Update ready: restart** in **HyperBrowser ▾**. Your identity, your sites and your settings stay as they are.

To run it from its [source code](https://github.com/tibocub/HyperBrowser) instead (Node.js 22 or newer, git): put `hypergraph`, `SwarmFS` and `HyperBrowser` side by side, run `npm install` in each (hypergraph and SwarmFS first), then `npm start` in `HyperBrowser`.

## The window

HyperBrowser draws its own window. At the top:

- **HyperBrowser ▾**: your identity, your installed sites, open a site by key, the look (system, light or dark, and the accent colour), and what closing the window does.
- **The tabs**, one per open site. The desktop is always the first tab. `+` opens a new one.
- Under them, **the toolbar**: back, forward, reload, the address, the bookmark button ☆, and the site's state.

A site only ever draws below these bars. Anything above them is HyperBrowser itself.

## Closing the window

Closing the window doesn't stop HyperBrowser: it stays in the tray (by the clock) so your computer keeps hosting your installed sites, your own sites and the forums you joined. Right-click its icon to open the window again, see how many sites it hosts, or **Quit**.

Rather have it quit? In **HyperBrowser ▾**, under "When the window closes", choose **Quit**.

On Linux, the tray depends on your desktop (GNOME needs the AppIndicator extension). Without one, closing the window quits.

## Open a site by its key

A site's key is 52 letters and digits. Paste it in the address and press Enter. The address then shows the site's name and a short key, like `HyperBrowser · 6x99a3…cjy`; click it to see the whole key.

If nobody who has the site is online, the tab says so: try again later.

## Tabs

Each site opens in its own tab, and keeps running when you switch to another one.

| Keys | |
|---|---|
| Ctrl+T | the desktop, address ready |
| Ctrl+W | close the tab |
| Ctrl+Tab, Ctrl+Shift+Tab | next, previous tab |
| Ctrl+L | the address |
| F5, Ctrl+R | reload |
| Alt+Left, Alt+Right | back, forward |

## The desktop

The desktop is your home: each site's icon and name. Double-click an icon (or press Enter) to open it. Right-click it to install it, make it a bookmark, or remove it. Drag icons to arrange them.

The official site (this one) and the official forum are already there.

## Install or bookmark

There is only one kind of thing in HyperBrowser: a site. You keep it in one of two ways:

- **Installed** (solid frame): every page and file of the site is kept on your computer and never thrown away. Your computer serves it to others, even when the site isn't open.
- **Bookmark** (dotted frame): an icon to open the site quickly. Its pages are kept for a while, then thrown away to make room. Your computer serves it to others while it's open, and for a short time after you close it.

The ☆ button in the toolbar adds or removes a bookmark.

## What "hosted" means

There is no server. A site is **hosted** by every computer that has it and is online: its owner's, and its visitors'. The more people keep a site, the easier it is to reach.

The right of the toolbar shows the site's state:

- `■ complete` or `□ loading`: whether your computer has the whole site
- how many **peers** (other computers with the site) you are connected to
- how much of the site your computer holds, and `kept` when the site is installed

If the owner is offline and nobody else who has the site is online, the site can't be reached until someone comes back.
